description:
Found by s533d (1c port 2) while triaging its D0 F row eval.t:151 `enclosing:$got`.

Probe (scratch/s533d/pr/E4.pl in the s533d worktree):

    {
        my $got;
        sub rec { my $l = shift; if ($l < 3) { eval 'rec($l + 1)'; } else { $got = "ok $l" } }
        rec(0);
        print "in=$got\n";
    }
    print "between=", (defined $got ? $got : 'u'), "\n";

perl 5.40.3:  in=ok 3 / between=u
base 0f2db817 (and s533d's tree):  in=ok 3 / between=ok 3   -- SILENT WRONG

Mechanism: `_promote_captured` promotes the block's `my $got` (captured by the named sub `rec`) to `$got__file__N`; with a string eval after the decl and no ENCLOSING same-name lexical, `$eval_pair` is true and the M-F alias rule (`_reg_eval_capture` -> `p-alias-eval-cell '$got $got__file__N`, ir-spec §9.1) writes the cell into the ORIGINAL-name global `$main::got` at the decl's run position ("time-ordered like v1's defvar model").  The alias outlives the block, so the global `$got` read after the block (perl: the package variable, undef) sees the cell.  The per-site pair path (`_eval_block_cells`, M5) that the "enclosing same-name lexical" shape takes is block-scoped and does not leak.

s533d did NOT widen it: its port keeps eval.t:151 / mro basic.t:93 (a same-name FILE `my` declared AFTER the block) on the per-site pair path (Binder::enclosing_decl counts a same-name `my` whose SCOPE encloses the extent, wherever in that scope -- guard row 11 of Pl/t/capture-binder-01.t).  Its first D1 cut (the pad's answer: only a binding VISIBLE at the extent) took the alias path there and printed `between=ok 3` for eval.t's shape -- that is how this was found.

Fix direction (not designed): a BLOCK-extent scalar promotion with a later string eval could always take the per-site pair (the M5 path) instead of the global alias; the alias is only right for a SEGMENT-extent (file-level) cell, whose lifetime is the file's.  Measure with corpus-diff (every block-extent promotion with a post-decl eval moves).


